@database "terminal"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: terminal.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Terminal"
@{h1}Terminal

The terminal gives an agent a shell: it runs a command and reads what came
out, waits for it or lets it run in the background, reads the output of
what runs there later and ends it. Every command runs in a fresh
@{tt}bash -c@{utt} -- Git Bash on Windows. A plain terminal runs whatever
the agent asks for, with the rights and the environment of the server;
a whitelist narrows it to named commands, and confinement keeps its
writes inside one directory on Linux and macOS. Read
@{" What an agent can run " link security} before you give an agent one.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  What an agent can run  " link security}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Running a command  " link execute}
  @{"  Background processes  " link background}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node execute "Running a command"
@{h1}Running a command

The plugin gives an agent three tools, named after the instance:
@{tt}terminal_execute@{utt}, @{tt}terminal_get_output@{utt} and
@{tt}terminal_kill_process@{utt} in the default setup. @{tt}execute@{utt}
runs a command; with @{tt}background: true@{utt} it starts it and answers at
once (see @{" Background processes " link background}).

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Used by | Meaning
command | always | the shell line, run as @{tt}bash -c <command>@{utt}; required
background | always | true: start the command and answer at once; default false
timeout | foreground | seconds, from 1 to max_timeout_seconds (3600); left out or null: default_timeout_seconds (300)
cwd | both | the directory to run in; a relative path is taken from the start directory. Not offered on a whitelisted terminal
env_vars | both | variables laid over the server's environment. Not offered on a whitelisted terminal
process_id | background | a name of your own for the process, instead of @{tt}bg_proc_<8 hex digits>@{utt}
wake | background | wake this session when the process ends
@{body}

Each call is a new shell: a @{tt}cd@{utt} or an @{tt}export@{utt} does not
carry over to the next command. The start directory is
@{tt}platform.initial_cwd@{utt}, or the directory the server was started
from (see @{" Setting it up " link setup}). A directory that does not
exist fails the call with the system's error.

@{h2}The answer

@{table}
Field | Meaning
status | success when the command ran -- also with a non-zero exit code; error when it did not run or timed out; cancelled when the run was stopped
exit_code | the shell's exit code
stdout | what the command wrote, stdout and stderr merged in the order they came
stderr | always empty in the foreground (merged into stdout)
truncated | true when the output was longer than max_output_size_kb
execution_time | seconds
cwd, command | where and what ran
@{body}

Output is capped at @{tt}max_output_size_kb@{utt} (60 KB): the first 60 KB
are kept, the rest is read and dropped, and the text ends with
"... [OUTPUT TRUNCATED - exceeded size limit. Use filters like 'head',
'tail', 'grep' to limit output]".

A timeout kills the command and everything it started, and answers
@{tt}error_type: TimeoutError@{utt} ("Command timeout after 30s"), with
the output the command had written until then in @{tt}stdout@{utt} (and
@{tt}truncated@{utt} as above). A Stop or Ctrl+C that cancels the agent's
run ends the command the same way, at once, and the call answers
@{tt}status: cancelled@{utt} (@{tt}error_type: Cancelled@{utt}), with the
output so far as well. Should the framework force-cancel the call instead
(after the agent's @{tt}timeouts.tool_cleanup_timeout@{utt}, 30 s by
default), the command is killed with it, and there is no answer to carry
the output.

"Everything it started" has two exceptions: on Linux and macOS a process
that leaves the command's process group (@{tt}setsid@{utt}, a daemon that
detaches itself), and on Windows a child the shell starts in the instant
before it is put into its job object.

@{h2}Errors

@{table}
error_type | When
SecurityError | the command is refused: a built-in or configured pattern, the whitelist, or a second command where chains are off
ConfiguredOnly | cwd or env_vars sent to a whitelisted terminal; nothing is started
InvalidTimeout | timeout is no number, or less than 1
TimeoutExceeded | timeout above max_timeout_seconds
TimeoutError | the command ran longer than its timeout
Cancelled | the agent's run was stopped while the command ran (status: cancelled)
SandboxUnavailable | a confinement mode is set and this host cannot enforce it
@{body}

A refused call starts nothing. Any other failure to start (a missing
directory, a bad environment value) answers @{tt}status: error@{utt} with
the exception's name as @{tt}error_type@{utt}.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"command": "git status --short", "timeout": 30}
{"command": "pytest -q tests/unit", "cwd": "backend", "timeout": 600}
{"command": "npm run dev", "background": true, "process_id": "dev"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node background "Background processes"
@{h1}Background processes

@{tt}execute@{utt} with @{tt}background: true@{utt} starts the command and
answers at once with @{tt}process_id@{utt}, @{tt}pid@{utt},
@{tt}command@{utt}, @{tt}cwd@{utt} and @{tt}started_at@{utt}. No timeout
applies: the process runs until it ends, is killed, or the server stops.
Stopping the plugin kills every background process whose shell still runs,
with what it started. A process whose shell has already ended is skipped,
so what it left running (@{tt}nohup ... &@{utt}, a detached daemon)
survives the server.

@{h2}Limits

@{bullet}At most @{tt}max_concurrent_background@{utt} run at once per
session (10 unless the server entry says otherwise; the coder's
@{tt}coder_shell@{utt} has 5). Another session's processes do not count:
they are invisible to this one and it could not end them. One more is
refused with @{tt}TooManyProcesses@{utt}, naming how many are this
session's, which @{tt}kill_process@{utt} can end.
@{bullet}Over all sessions together, one terminal instance runs at most four
times that limit; past it every session is refused, with a message that
says how many of the running ones are its own.
@{bullet}Each stream keeps its last 1000 lines. A line longer than 64 KiB
is kept in pieces.
@{bullet}A finished process stays in memory, with its output, until the
server stops or its id is used again.

@{h2}Reading the output: get_output

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
process_id | the id execute returned
stream | stdout, stderr or both (default); anything else answers InvalidStream
clear_buffer | true: empty the returned streams' buffers, so the next call returns only newer output
@{body}

The answer has @{tt}stdout@{utt} and @{tt}stderr@{utt} apart (unlike the
foreground), @{tt}is_running@{utt}, @{tt}exit_code@{utt} (null while it
runs), @{tt}started_at@{utt} and @{tt}finished_at@{utt}. Each stream
returns at most its last @{tt}max_output_size_kb@{utt} (60 K characters);
@{tt}truncated: true@{utt} says the start was cut. With
@{tt}clear_buffer@{utt} the cut part is cleared too.

@{h2}Ending it: kill_process

@{tt}process_id@{utt}, and @{tt}force@{utt} (default false). On Linux and
macOS the process group -- the shell and everything it started -- gets
SIGTERM; if the shell and its output pipes are not done 5 seconds later,
the group gets SIGKILL (the answer then says "SIGKILL (forced)"). The wait
is on the shell: a child that ignores SIGTERM and writes elsewhere than
the pipes (@{tt}> file@{utt}) lets the shell end in time and gets no
SIGKILL. @{tt}force: true@{utt} sends SIGKILL to the group at once. On
Windows both end the process and everything it started at once. The answer:
@{tt}killed: true@{utt}, @{tt}signal@{utt}, @{tt}exit_code@{utt}. A process
that has already ended answers @{tt}ProcessAlreadyTerminated@{utt}.

@{h2}Whose process it is

A process belongs to the session that started it. For any other session it
does not exist: get_output and kill_process answer @{tt}ProcessNotFound@{utt}
("Process dev not found"), exactly as for an id nobody used.

@{h2}Choosing the id

An id of your own (@{tt}"dev"@{utt}, @{tt}"build"@{utt}) is refused with
@{tt}ProcessIdInUse@{utt} while a process under it runs, while another call
is starting one under it, or when it belongs to another session. Once the
process under it has ended, the next start takes the name over, and the
old output and any recorded result go with it.

@{h2}Waking instead of polling

With @{tt}wake: true@{utt} the session is woken when the process ends --
finished or failed -- so the agent can end its turn instead of polling. The
answer says whether that is armed: @{tt}"wake": true@{utt}, or
@{tt}"wake": false@{utt} with a @{tt}wake_note@{utt} naming why:

@{table}
wake_note | Meaning
session presence is off (config: session_presence.enabled) | nobody can be woken
this call belongs to no session, so there is nobody to wake | an internal call
waking is switched off (config: session_presence.max_wake_depth is 0) | switched off
this run was itself woken, N deep, and the wake chain stops at max_wake_depth=M | a chain of runs woken by woken runs stops there
wake applies to background=true only; ... | a foreground call: its result is in the answer
@{body}

A call without @{tt}wake@{utt} gets neither field. An armed wake is best
effort: a one-shot @{tt}agent-cli run@{utt} ends its turn and takes its
background processes with it, although the answer said the wake is armed. When the process ends the core is told that
input is waiting; a session busy in a turn is rung again every 10 seconds,
for up to five minutes, until it reads the result with get_output or kills
the process.

A woken session may be continued by a new process that never had the
background process in memory. So an armed wake also writes the outcome --
exit code and the last 30 000 characters of each stream -- to the plugin's
cache, for one hour. get_output answers from there when the process is not
in memory, marks the answer @{tt}"source": "recorded"@{utt}, and deletes
the record: it is read once.
@endnode

@node security "What an agent can run"
@{h1}What an agent can run

@{h2}Everything the server can

A plain terminal -- no whitelist, no confinement, the default -- runs any
command as the user the server runs as, in any directory, with any
environment. Its pattern lists are a hand-brake against slips, not a
boundary. What keeps an agent from the shell is whether it gets the tool
at all: its allowlist (@{tt}+terminal/*@{utt}), its role gate
(@{tt}metadata.min_role@{utt}; the shipped agents with a full terminal are
admin-only), and, if you want every command asked for, the
@{tt}tool_approval@{utt} hook.

@{h2}The shell

Every call is a new @{tt}bash -c@{utt}; a command may chain, pipe and
substitute as bash allows unless chains are off. The bash is found once,
at start:

@{table}
Platform | Order
Windows | C:\\Program Files\\Git\\bin\\bash.exe if it exists, else the first bash on PATH; none: the terminal does not load
Linux, macOS | the first bash on PATH, else /bin/bash
@{body}

@{tt}platform.bash_path@{utt} names one instead. On Windows the first bash
on PATH may be WSL's (@{tt}System32\\bash.exe@{utt}), which runs the
command in Linux; set @{tt}bash_path@{utt} to Git Bash if Git is installed
elsewhere. From Git Bash, @{tt}cmd //c ...@{utt} and
@{tt}powershell -c ...@{utt} are programs like any other: no list stops
them unless it names them.

@{h2}The environment and secrets

A command gets the server's environment, @{tt}env_vars@{utt} laid over it
-- without the variables the server took from its secrets files
(@{tt}config/secrets.env@{utt} and the local one). A variable set by the
real environment (systemd, the shell that started the server) is passed
on, key or not. The list the server keeps of the file's variables
(@{tt}HIVE_SECRETS_FROM_FILE@{utt}: each name with a short fingerprint of
its value, enough to confirm a guessed short secret) is left out too.
@{tt}security.pass_secrets_env: true@{utt} passes all of them, as before.

This keeps keys out of @{tt}env@{utt} output, out of a build log that
prints its environment and out of the model's context by accident. It is
not a boundary: reads are not confined, so @{tt}cat config/secrets.env@{utt}
still shows them, confined or not. Only a whitelist that names no program
able to read that file keeps an agent from it. Commands get no input of their own: they inherit the server's
standard input, so give them what they need as arguments or files, and use
non-interactive flags.

@{h2}The pattern lists

Asked in this order; the first that refuses answers
@{tt}SecurityError@{utt} with the pattern:

@{bullet}@{b}The built-in list@{ub}, searched anywhere in the command,
ignoring case: @{tt}rm -rf /@{utt}, @{tt}dd if=... of=/dev/@{utt}, a fork
bomb, @{tt}mkfs.@{utt}, @{tt}chmod -R 777@{utt}, @{tt}chown -R root@{utt},
@{tt}wget ... | sh@{utt}, @{tt}curl ... | bash@{utt}.
@{tt}security.dangerous_patterns@{utt} adds to it.
@{bullet}@{b}The whitelist@{ub}, when set: see below.
@{bullet}@{b}security.blacklist@{ub}: regexes, searched anywhere, ignoring
case.
@{bullet}@{b}One command per call@{ub}, when
@{tt}allow_command_chains@{utt} is false or a whitelist is set.

A list matches spellings, not intentions. Measured against the built-in
list and the blacklist of the shipped configuration: @{tt}rm -fr /@{utt},
@{tt}rm -r -f /@{utt}, @{tt}rm -rf C:/@{utt}, @{tt}r''m -rf /@{utt}, a
variable holding the path, @{tt}chmod 0777@{utt}, a download written to a
file and run next, and @{tt}cmd //c rd /s /q ...@{utt} all pass. The other
way round, the text is searched without regard to quoting:
@{tt}echo 'rm -rf /'@{utt} and @{tt}grep mkfs notes.txt@{utt} are refused.

The coder's @{tt}coder_shell@{utt} blacklists the git verbs that rewrite
history or throw work away (@{tt}git push@{utt}, @{tt}git reset --hard@{utt},
@{tt}git checkout --@{utt}, @{tt}git clean -@{utt}). Measured, these pass
it: @{tt}git  push@{utt} with two spaces, @{tt}git -C . push@{utt},
@{tt}git reset  --hard@{utt}, @{tt}git restore .@{utt},
@{tt}git switch -f@{utt} and @{tt}git stash drop@{utt}. It stops a model
that types the usual spelling by habit, nothing more.

@{h2}One command per call

With chains off, a command is refused when it contains a line break,
@{tt};@{utt}, @{tt}&@{utt}, a pipe character, a backtick, @{tt}$(@{utt},
@{tt}<(@{utt} or @{tt}>(@{utt} anywhere -- inside quotes as well, since
telling a quoted one from a live one takes a shell parser. That keeps the
shell line to one command. It does not stop a command that runs another
from its own arguments: @{tt}eval@{utt}, @{tt}bash -c@{utt} or
@{tt}python -c@{utt} with a quoted argument that holds none of those
characters. Redirections (@{tt}> file@{utt}) stay allowed.

@{h2}A whitelist

With @{tt}security.whitelist@{utt} set, a command runs only when one of
its regexes matches from the first character. Such a terminal is a
boundary, as far as its patterns are:

@{bullet}every command runs in the configured start directory and
environment: the tool does not offer @{tt}cwd@{utt} and
@{tt}env_vars@{utt}, and refuses them (@{tt}ConfiguredOnly@{utt}) in the
foreground and the background alike;
@{bullet}one command per call, whatever @{tt}allow_command_chains@{utt}
says;
@{bullet}no control character reaches a pattern: anything below 0x20 but
tab, and DEL, is refused first.

Writing the patterns:

@{bullet}Anchor them at both ends: @{tt}^...\\Z@{utt}. @{tt}$@{utt} also
matches before a final line break, and a pattern without an end lets
anything follow.
@{bullet}Name the program exactly: @{tt}^python ...@{utt}, not
@{tt}^.*python ...@{utt}. A path to it in full, relative to
@{tt}initial_cwd@{utt}, with forward slashes: bash drops unquoted
backslashes.
@{bullet}Separate words with literal spaces, not @{tt}\\s@{utt}, which also
matches a line break.
@{bullet}Name a program that does not run other commands from its
arguments, and arguments that cannot carry a path or an option you did
not mean: the whitelist checks the text, what the program does with it is
up to the program. @{tt}^git .*@{utt} allows every git alias and hook.
@{bullet}Set @{tt}platform.initial_cwd@{utt} when the commands name
relative paths.

@{code yaml}
security:
  whitelist:
    - '^python tools/report\\.py (daily|weekly)\\Z'
  allow_command_chains: false
platform:
  initial_cwd: /srv/reports
@{body}

@{h2}Confinement

@{tt}sandbox.mode@{utt} hands the command to the kernel already unable to
write outside a workspace, whatever it runs:

@{table}
Mode | The command may write
danger-full-access | anywhere the server may; the default
workspace-write | under @{tt}sandbox.workspace_root@{utt} (default: the start directory) and a temp directory
read-only | only a temp directory
@{body}

@{table}
Platform | Backend | Temp directory
Linux | bubblewrap (install it: @{tt}apt install bubblewrap@{utt}) | a private /tmp that vanishes with the command
macOS | Seatbelt (@{tt}/usr/bin/sandbox-exec@{utt}) | the user's $TMPDIR, shared with the user's other programs (partial confinement)
Windows | none | --
@{body}

A mode that cannot be enforced refuses every command
(@{tt}SandboxUnavailable@{utt}) instead of running it unconfined -- on
Windows always, on Linux without bubblewrap. Confinement covers file
writes only: reads, the network and the environment stay as they are. In @{tt}workspace-write@{utt} the
git hooks and config of the workspace's repository are read-only too. The
backends' details and gaps are in
@{tt}src/agent_system/utils/process_sandbox.py@{utt} -- among them: a
confined command can still ask an unconfined process over its socket (a
tmux server, an ssh agent) to act for it, and an unconfined tool that
writes into the workspace follows the symlinks a confined command left
there.

@{h2}Time, output and processes

@{bullet}A foreground command is killed at its timeout (300 s by default,
at most 3600 s), with everything it started.
@{bullet}Output is capped at 60 KB per answer; the rest is read and
dropped, not held in memory.
@{bullet}Background processes have no timeout; at most
@{tt}max_concurrent_background@{utt} (10 by default) run at once per
session, four times that over all sessions. Stopping the server ends those
still running.
@{bullet}Each command runs in a process group of its own on Linux and
macOS, and in a job object on Windows, so a timeout, a cancel or
kill_process ends what it started. On Linux and macOS this also takes it
off the server's terminal: a Ctrl+C there no longer reaches it directly,
and it has no controlling terminal to read from. There, a process that leaves its
group (@{tt}setsid@{utt}, a daemon) is not reached.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The three tool descriptions and their parameters come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, rendered once per instance: the timeout's default
and maximum are the configured ones. A whitelisted
terminal's @{tt}execute@{utt} has no @{tt}cwd@{utt} and no
@{tt}env_vars@{utt}, and its description adds "This terminal runs only
the commands its configuration allows, in its configured working directory
and environment." A confinement mode adds one sentence, for example on
Linux: "Commands run sandboxed (workspace-write): they can modify files
only under /srv/work and in a private /tmp; other writes fail with a
permission error, and so do writes to the git hooks and config of the
workspace's repository." Where no backend exists: "Commands are refused
here: sandbox mode workspace-write is set, and this host has no sandbox
backend."

@{h2}Refusals, word for word

@{bullet}"Command blocked: matches dangerous pattern 'rm\\s+-rf\\s+/'"
@{bullet}"Command blocked by blacklist pattern 'mkfs'"
@{bullet}"Command not in whitelist"
@{bullet}"Command blocked: this terminal runs one command per call, and ';'
starts or joins another one (command chains are off)" -- on a whitelisted
terminal "(its whitelist names single commands)"
@{bullet}"This terminal runs only the commands its configuration allows, in
its configured working directory and environment -- cwd cannot be set
here. Send the command again without cwd."
@{bullet}"timeout must be a number of seconds from 1 to 3600, got '30s'"
@{bullet}"Timeout 4000s exceeds maximum of 3600s"
@{bullet}"process_id dev is in use by a running process; choose another one
or leave it out"
@{bullet}"5 background processes of this session are running, the limit is 5
per session; kill_process one of them or wait for one to end"
@{bullet}"20 background processes are running on this terminal, the cap for all
sessions together is 20; none of them is this session's, or wait for one to
end"

A denied write under confinement is no plugin error: the command fails on
its own, with a non-zero exit code and the system's message in its output.

@{h2}The status line

@{bullet}Foreground: "Executing: <command>", then "<command> -- exit 0,
0.42s, 12 lines" ("12+ lines (truncated)", "no output"), or
"<command> failed: <error>".
@{bullet}Background: "Background process dev started (PID 4242) (wakes this
session): npm run dev".
@{bullet}get_output: "Output retrieved from process 'dev' (running): 5120
bytes stdout, 0 bytes stderr" -- the figures count characters.
@{bullet}kill_process: "Process 'dev' terminated with SIGTERM".

The command is folded to one line and shortened to 70 characters there;
in the background line the id gets 30 and the command what is left of 138.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The tool definitions are the same on every request, so the prompt cache
holds; the plugin adds nothing to the system prompt. Answers are ordinary
tool results, appended. A foreground answer carries up to 60 KB of output,
a get_output answer up to 60 K characters per stream -- a long build log
read with @{tt}stream: both@{utt} is two of them. Filter in the command
(@{tt}| tail -50@{utt}, @{tt}grep@{utt}) where chains are allowed, and
read one stream at a time.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}A call the framework force-cancels ends without an answer, so the
output of its command is lost. Run a long command in the background to
keep what it wrote whatever happens to the call.
@{bullet}A foreground answer keeps the start of a long output, get_output
the end.
@{bullet}Finished background processes stay in memory until the server
stops or their id is reused.
@{bullet}The pattern lists are not a boundary, and a whitelist is only as
narrow as its patterns.
@{bullet}Credentials set by the real environment reach every command, and
the secrets file stays readable to it.
@{bullet}Windows has no confinement backend.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The default configuration has one terminal:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    terminal:
      type: terminal
      enabled: true
      security:
        blacklist:
          - "rm -rf /"
          - "dd if="
          - "mkfs"
          - "chmod 777"
        allow_command_chains: true
      limits:
        max_output_size_kb: 60
        default_timeout_seconds: 300
        max_timeout_seconds: 3600
        max_concurrent_background: 10
      platform:
        bash_path: "auto"
        initial_cwd: null
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+terminal/*"
@{body}

It needs bash: Git Bash on Windows, bubblewrap on Linux for
confinement. No Python packages beyond the core.

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default in code | Meaning
security.whitelist | none | regexes; when set, only matching commands run (see @{" A whitelist " link security})
security.blacklist | none | regexes refused on top of the built-in list
security.dangerous_patterns | none | regexes added to the built-in list
security.allow_command_chains | true | false: one command per call
security.pass_secrets_env | false | true: commands also get the variables taken from the secrets files
limits.max_output_size_kb | 60 | output cap per answer, and per stream in get_output
limits.default_timeout_seconds | 300 | a foreground command's timeout when the call gives none
limits.max_timeout_seconds | 3600 | the largest timeout a call may ask for
limits.max_concurrent_background | 10 | background processes running at once per session; four times it over all sessions
platform.bash_path | auto | the bash to run; auto finds one (see @{" The shell " link security})
platform.initial_cwd | the directory the server was started from | where commands run; relative to the server's working directory
sandbox.mode | danger-full-access | read-only, workspace-write or danger-full-access; an unknown mode keeps the instance from loading
sandbox.workspace_root | the start directory | what workspace-write may write to
@{body}

The @{tt}config:@{utt} block in the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} only
documents these; the defaults above are what the code applies when the
server entry leaves a key out. Without an @{tt}initial_cwd@{utt},
agent-cli and agent-run take the directory you started them in; the API,
the directory it was started from.

The terminal keeps nothing on disk but the results an armed wake records:
one file per process in the plugin cache (@{tt}data/cache/<instance>/@{utt}),
for one hour or until it is read.
@endnode
